# Prettier reads .gitignore by default, which covers the root build output
# (/.next, /coverage, /out, /build) and node_modules. This file exists for what
# .gitignore CANNOT cover.
#
# ★★ A CLAUDE CODE WORKTREE IS A FULL SECOND CHECKOUT, and it is excluded from
# git via .git/info/exclude — a LOCAL-ONLY mechanism Prettier cannot see. So
# `prettier --write .` walked into it and reformatted a few thousand generated
# files under `.claude/worktrees/*/.next/`.
#
# ★ AND `--write` MAKES THAT MORE THAN SLOW: a worktree is another branch's
# working tree, so formatting it rewrites files belonging to work in progress
# somewhere else, invisibly, with no diff in this checkout to show for it.
#
# ⚠ CI NEVER SEES THIS. `.claude/worktrees/` is git-excluded, so only the local
# run breaks — the one a person actually reads. `eslint.config.mjs` records the
# identical failure from 2026-08-10, when a worktree's build output turned
# `npm run lint` into 65,172 problems. `tsconfig.json` is immune for a different
# reason: TypeScript's `**` globs skip dot-directories, so `.claude` is never
# descended into.
.claude/worktrees/

# ★ `**/` IS LOAD-BEARING, exactly as it is in eslint.config.mjs. .gitignore's
# entries are root-ANCHORED (`/.next/`, `/coverage`), so they match the build
# output here and nowhere else. These catch generated output in any nested
# checkout, which should never be formatted wherever it sits.
**/.next/
**/coverage/
